## How to read skills
**Read content**: `read_skills(skill_names=["skill-name"])`
**Read a referenced-project companion skill**: companion skills live only on the source project named by the `@micro_app` or `@project` mention, not in the current workspace (including a micro-app runtime project). When loading that companion skill, the first `read_skills` for it must copy `project_id` from the mention: `read_skills(skill_names=["skill-name"], project_id="...")`. Do not omit or invent `project_id` on that companion-skill load, do not load a same-named workspace or system skill, and do not search the current workspace `.magic/skills/` with list_dir, read_files, or a guessed mount path. System and current-workspace skills still omit `project_id`.
**Read related files**: The skill content lists related file paths (relative to the skill directory). When using `read_files`, always construct an absolute path. Prefer the absolute skill directory from the `<skill_dir>` tag, then append the relative path given in the skill content. If `<skill_dir>` is unavailable, take the absolute path from the `<location>` tag and strip the filename to get the skill directory.
Example: `<skill_dir>/abs/path/to/skill-name</skill_dir>` + `reference/doc.md` from skill content → `read_files(files=[{"file_path": "/abs/path/to/skill-name/reference/doc.md"}])`
**Never** use a relative path. Copy the file name and subdirectory exactly as written in the skill content — do not guess or infer any part of the path.
After reading, if another skill is also needed, call `read_skills` again for that skill.

Read results preserve the source file, including frontmatter and comments. Omit displayed line-number prefixes when copying text into edits.
An oversized Skill requires informed user consent before further reading: explain that context compaction may summarize earlier conversation and tool results and lose some details. No response or refusal is not consent; do not ask again for each continuation page after consent for that Skill.
When a relevant Skill file changes, use the file change information. If the notice only provides a summary or the current instructions are unclear, reload its main instructions with `read_skills` or its reference file with `read_files`. Do not infer current instructions from a summary.

